#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The RESPONSE half of publish-before-flip (.github#748, epic #266).

`check-feed-coherence.py` (.github#267) is the DETECTION half: it reads the live org feed and goes
RED on `main` when a release published and `registry/dependencies.yml` was never flipped. Detection
is sound — it caught the drift at #250, #263, and four more times since. What was missing is
RESPONSE: nothing turned "a release published" into "a `.github` PR flipping the row exists". Every
one of the SEVEN recorded instances was reconciled BY HAND, and the count grew rather than
converged (#250 -> #263 -> the missed 0.4.0-preview.1 -> the unrecorded 0.4.2 -> 0.9.2 -> 0.10.0
(#747) -> 0.11.0 (#856)).

This runs the flip the humans were running, and opens the PR they were opening.

WHY THE FEED, NOT THE DISPATCH. #748 proposes that FS.GG.Rendering `repository_dispatch` this repo
on release, and that a receiver here verify-and-flip. The receiver is real (feed-autofix.yml listens
for it), but the dispatch is a HINT ABOUT WHEN TO LOOK — never the source of truth. Three reasons,
in ascending order of seriousness:

  1. It cannot land alone. The sender is a PR in ANOTHER repo, so a dispatch-only response half is
     inert until that lands, and "the receiver never ran" and "there was nothing to do" would be the
     same silence — the exact #266 shape this exists to close.
  2. The org already answered this question the other way, for a structurally identical problem.
     `skill-registry-autofix.yml` is the response half of `skill-registry-coherence.yml`, and its
     header says why it polls: "the simple version is the only one that lives entirely in `.github`".
  3. THE DISPATCH FABRIC HAS ITSELF FAILED TO FIRE, TWICE, AND THE REGISTRY RECORDS BOTH. The 0.5.0
     release went out via a manual `release.yml` workflow_dispatch, which does not fire
     `template-dispatch.yml` at all ("FS.GG.Templates was NOT auto-notified ... re-fire owed"); and
     at 0.4.0 the dispatch fired and was WRONG, passing the tag ref through `env: GITHUB_REF:`, a
     name the runner reserves and re-injects (fixed in FS.GG.Rendering#232). A response half keyed
     ONLY on the dispatch inherits every way the sender can fail to fire — which is the manual step
     it replaces, moved one level down and made harder to see.

So the feed is the subject on every trigger. A dispatch makes the flip land in minutes instead of
within a day; it never decides WHAT the flip is. That also means this bot is correct for a release
nobody dispatched, a hand-published one, and a re-publish.

IT MUST NOT BLIND-WRITE (#748). The registry's whole value is that its provenance is CHECKED rather
than inferred, so every write here is backed by an assertion, and every assertion FAILS CLOSED:

  * FEED, PACKAGE-BY-PACKAGE. The template package alone cannot distinguish a FRAMEWORK release
    (the libs moved; the `version` pin advances with the package) from a TEMPLATE-ONLY cut (only
    template/ content moved; the pin HOLDS and the two fields decouple — 0.3.1-preview.1, 0.4.1).
    Getting that wrong writes a pin no consumer can restore. So the template's 17 sibling members
    are read and compared INDIVIDUALLY (the template itself is the subject being classified, not
    evidence for it); a PARTIAL publish — some siblings at the new version, some not — is a real
    defect and is reported, never averaged away into a guess.
  * A TAG IS NOT A PUBLISH, and `package-tag` is the field that would carry that lie. The phantom
    0.9.1 is three live tags backed by ZERO packages: `release-tags` pushed the triple from a job
    that succeeded, both release-only gates then failed, and publish was correctly skipped — so the
    tags outlived the publish. This bot writes `package-tag` only for a version the FEED serves, and
    only when the tag it names actually exists and the triple resolves to ONE commit.

WHY THERE IS NO "the publish job succeeded" ASSERTION, WHICH #748 ALSO LISTS. Because the feed read
above is STRICTLY STRONGER EVIDENCE OF THE SAME FACT, and this bot can only ever flip to a version
the feed already serves. "The release run says it published" is a self-report; "the package is
downloadable at that version, and so are its 17 siblings" is the thing the self-report is evidence
FOR. The 0.9.1 phantom is precisely a run whose tags exist and whose packages do not — and it is
caught here by the feed, not by the run. Adding the run read would cost a cross-repo `actions: read`
scope to re-derive a fact already in hand, and a second way to fail.

THE PROSE STAYS HUMAN (#748). A bot must not write "what makes this a major" — that is the judgement
the row exists to record. It writes the three MECHANICAL fields and nothing else.

But the prose is not therefore safe to ignore, because it ROTS SILENTLY and the last hand-flip
proves it: #856 moved `package-version` to 0.11.0 and left its comment reading "ADVANCED 0.9.2 ->
0.10.0 ... (pin == package == 0.10.0)", and left `package-tag` at v0.10.0 entirely. Nothing went
red — no gate reads `package-tag`, and `check-feed-coherence` reads the VALUE, never the comment. So
this bot REPORTS the comments its own write has contradicted (`--json` carries them; the PR body
lists them as a checklist). It does not edit them. Flagging is not fixing, and is not meant to be:
it puts the judgement in front of the human who has it, which is the one thing the silence never did.

Pure stdlib + PyYAML. The feed/nuspec/tag readers are INJECTED (`resolve_feed`, `resolve_nuspec`,
`resolve_tag`), never reached for globally, so tests/feed-autofix drives every branch offline.
`--fixture` refuses to run unless FSGG_FEED_FIXTURE_OK=1 — a test hook that can silently turn the
response half into a no-op is the very defect class above.

Exit 0 = reconciled (or already coherent). Exit 1 = a finding a human must settle. Exit 2 = a defect.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import sys

import yaml

# `scripts/` is not a package; the test harness loads this by path via importlib, which sets
# sys.path[0] to the TEST's directory rather than here. Mirrors check-feed-coherence.py.
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))

from fsgg_feed import (  # noqa: E402  (path shim above must run first)
    GateError,
    feed_versions,
    is_prerelease,
    newest,
    nuspec_dependency_ids,
    package_nuspec,
    parse_version,
)

# The repo that owns the fs-gg-ui coherent set and cuts its tags.
PRODUCER = "FS-GG/FS.GG.Rendering"

# The contract this bot reconciles. Deliberately ONE row, not "every row carrying a package-version".
#
# The other package-bearing contracts (fsgg-contracts, game-sim-core, fs-gg-audio, ...) have a
# SINGLE version field whose flip is a one-line value swap. `fs-gg-ui-template` is the only row
# whose flip requires a JUDGEMENT the feed can settle — framework release vs template-only cut, i.e.
# whether `version` moves with `package-version` or holds — and it is the row that has drifted all
# seven times. A bot that reconciled every row would be inventing a mechanism for six rows that do
# not need one; when one of them drifts, widen this deliberately.
CONTRACT = "fs-gg-ui-template"

TEMPLATE_PACKAGE = "FS.GG.UI.Template"

# The BOM / metapackage that DECLARES the coherent set. See `derive_members`.
BOM_PACKAGE = "FS.GG.UI"


class Finding(Exception):
    """A condition a human must settle. Never auto-resolved — see the header."""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The FS.GG.UI.* coherent set: every member a FRAMEWORK release republishes at one version.
#
# Knowing this set is what makes "framework release" a MEASURED claim rather than an inferred one.
# The registry's own prose records it being checked by hand every time ("verified against the org
# feed PACKAGE-BY-PACKAGE, not inferred from the template alone"). This is that check, run by a
# machine. FS.GG.UI.Template is excluded — it is the subject being classified, not evidence.
#
# The set is DERIVED, from the BOM's own nuspec, rather than hand-maintained here. It used to be a
# 17-id literal, and that literal was a silent bound (.github#933): if FS.GG.Rendering published an
# 18th member, a partial publish that left ONLY the new member behind classified as a clean
# framework release and flipped the pin to a version that member cannot serve. Every other shape was
# caught, so it was a narrow hole — but a SILENT one, which is the defect class this file exists to
# close.
#
# Three repairs were considered and rejected before this one (.github#933):
#
#   1. Read the producer's `src/` and diff it. Couples this bot to ANOTHER repo's private directory
#      layout, so a reorganisation there turns this into a permanently-red bot — and a check that
#      cries wolf teaches exactly one lesson, the one `skill-registry-autofix`'s header refuses to
#      teach. The mapping is not even mechanical: `src/Meta` packs as FS.GG.UI, and `src/Shared` /
#      `src/ColorPolicy` are source-included and pack as nothing.
#   2. Enumerate the feed's own ids. GitHub Packages does not serve an enumeration to a run-scoped
#      token; the REST packages API wants a classic PAT with `read:packages`, a stored org secret
#      and its own decision (`fsgg_feed.py` records why .github#267 avoided it).
#   3. Assert the count against the registry's prose. Prose is the thing this bot exists to distrust.
#
# The BOM makes option 2's cost unnecessary: the PAT was only ever needed to ENUMERATE the feed, and
# the set is a property of ONE package whose id we already know. `FS.GG.UI` "ships no assembly
# (exact [$version$] member deps only)" and "its membership is locked to the packable FS.GG.UI.* set
# by an always-on parity test" (registry/dependencies.yml, Feature 207) — so the org already
# publishes a gated, machine-readable declaration of exactly this set, reachable over the same
# authenticated path `feed_versions` already calls. That parity test is what makes the BOM
# trustworthy here: it is not a second hand-maintained list, it is a gated one.
#
# The BOM is read at its OWN newest version, NOT at the classification `candidate`. That distinction
# is load-bearing, and the obvious reading of it is a bug:
#
#   `candidate` is the newest FS.GG.UI.TEMPLATE, and a TEMPLATE-ONLY cut is defined by the members
#   deliberately NOT publishing at it. FS.GG.UI@<candidate> therefore does not exist on exactly the
#   path that must classify as template-only — measured, both cuts this bot's docstring names:
#   the feed serves FS.GG.UI.Template 0.4.1 and 0.3.1-preview.1, and no FS.GG.UI at either. Deriving
#   at `candidate` would 404 → Finding on every template-only cut, turning a legitimate release
#   shape into a permanently-red bot: the failure mode option 1 was rejected for, reintroduced by
#   the repair.
#
# The BOM's newest declares the CURRENT membership, which is the question ("what constitutes the
# framework?"), where the BOM at a template's version asks something that need not exist. It keeps
# the fail-closed property intact: a partial publish that leaves the BOM behind while its members
# move leaves the BOM itself `behind`, so the set splits and PARTIAL PUBLISH raises — the BOM is a
# member of its own set, and is measured like one.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def derive_members(resolve_feed, resolve_nuspec, stable_channel: bool) -> tuple[list[str], str]:
    """The coherent set, read off the BOM's nuspec. Returns (members, note). Never guesses.

    Returns the BOM's exact-pinned dependency ids PLUS the BOM's own id: a framework release
    republishes the BOM alongside its members, so the BOM is a member of the set it declares.

    There is deliberately NO fallback to a baked-in list. A stale hardcoded set is the bound this
    derivation exists to remove, so reinstating it as a default on failure would restore that bound
    at exactly the moment the derivation stopped working — and do it silently. If the BOM cannot be
    read, say so and stop (.github#933).
    """
    live = resolve_feed(BOM_PACKAGE)
    if stable_channel:
        stable = [v for v in live if not is_prerelease(v)]
        if not stable:
            raise Finding(
                f"the feed serves no stable {BOM_PACKAGE} — only prereleases {sorted(live)} — while "
                f"the registry tracks the stable channel. The coherent set is declared by the BOM, "
                f"and it cannot be derived from a BOM no consumer can restore."
            )
        live = stable
    bom_version = newest(live)

    try:
        nuspec = resolve_nuspec(BOM_PACKAGE, bom_version)
    except GateError as e:
        raise Finding(
            f"cannot read the {BOM_PACKAGE} BOM at {bom_version}, so the coherent set it declares "
            f"cannot be derived: {e}. The set is NOT assumed — classifying a release against a "
            f"guessed membership is how the pin ends up at a version some member cannot serve."
        ) from e

    members = nuspec_dependency_ids(nuspec)
    if not members:
        raise Finding(
            f"the {BOM_PACKAGE}@{bom_version} nuspec declares NO dependencies. The BOM is defined "
            f"as exact member deps only, so an empty set means the BOM was packed wrong — and an "
            f"empty coherent set would classify EVERY publish as a clean framework release. "
            f"Refusing."
        )

    # The BOM depends on its members; it cannot depend on itself, and it must not be omitted from
    # the set it declares — a framework release republishes it too.
    if BOM_PACKAGE not in members:
        members.insert(0, BOM_PACKAGE)

    # The template is the SUBJECT being classified, never evidence for its own classification. It is
    # not a BOM dependency today; if it ever becomes one, classifying the template against itself
    # would be trivially self-confirming, so drop it explicitly rather than rely on it staying out.
    members = [m for m in members if m != TEMPLATE_PACKAGE]
    note = (
        f"coherent set = {len(members)} ids derived from the {BOM_PACKAGE}@{bom_version} BOM nuspec"
    )
    return members, note


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading the row.
#
# The row is read with PyYAML but WRITTEN with a targeted text substitution (see `rewrite_field`).
# A load/dump round-trip would be far simpler and is not available: the fs-gg-ui-template row's
# three fields carry ~15KB of hand-written provenance in trailing `#` comments, and PyYAML discards
# every comment it parses. Dumping would silently delete the exact judgement the header promises to
# preserve — the write must therefore touch only the bytes between the quotes.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def load_row(path: str) -> dict:
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        doc = yaml.safe_load(fh)
    contracts = (doc or {}).get("contracts")
    if not isinstance(contracts, list):
        raise Finding(f"{path}: no `contracts:` list — the registry's shape changed.")
    for c in contracts:
        if isinstance(c, dict) and c.get("id") == CONTRACT:
            return c
    raise Finding(f"{path}: no contract with id {CONTRACT!r}. It was renamed or removed.")


def _require_str(row: dict, field: str) -> str:
    # A MISSING field and a MIS-TYPED one need different remedies, so they get different messages.
    # Collapsing them tells a human to "quote" a key that is not there, sending them to look for a
    # quoting bug that does not exist.
    if field not in row:
        raise Finding(
            f"{CONTRACT} has no `{field}` — the row lost a field this bot writes. It was renamed "
            f"or dropped; either way the flip cannot be recorded. Restore it, or retire this bot's "
            f"claim on it."
        )
    v = row[field]
    if not isinstance(v, str):
        # YAML coerces an unquoted 1.10 to the float 1.1 — the .github#267 trap, restated: a
        # bot that wrote a float back would corrupt the row it exists to repair.
        raise Finding(
            f"{CONTRACT}.{field} is {v!r} ({type(v).__name__}), not a quoted string. "
            f"Quote it — an unquoted version is a float, not a version."
        )
    return v


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Classification: framework release vs template-only cut. The one judgement the feed can settle.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def classify(
    candidate: str, resolve_feed, resolve_nuspec, stable_channel: bool
) -> tuple[str, list[str]]:
    """Return ("framework"|"template-only", notes) for a template published at `candidate`.

    FRAMEWORK    — every FS.GG.UI.* member's newest is `candidate`: the libs moved with it.
    TEMPLATE-ONLY — NO member is at `candidate`: only template/ content moved, and the members are
                   byte-identical, packed @pin as a --skip-duplicate no-op (0.3.1-preview.1, 0.4.1).

    Anything between those two is a PARTIAL publish — a real defect — and raises rather than
    guessing. Averaging it away is how a bot writes a pin no consumer can restore.

    The membership is derived from the BOM (see `derive_members`), so a member added upstream is
    picked up here without an edit to this file.
    """
    members, member_note = derive_members(resolve_feed, resolve_nuspec, stable_channel)
    at_candidate: list[str] = []
    behind: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    want = parse_version(candidate)

    for pkg in members:
        live = resolve_feed(pkg)
        if stable_channel:
            stable = [v for v in live if not is_prerelease(v)]
            if not stable:
                # NOT `or live`. Falling back to the prereleases would silently compare a stable
                # candidate against a channel the registry does not track, and this file claims to
                # fail closed. It is also the exact condition check-feed-coherence.py raises as a
                # problem in its own words ("the feed serves no stable version of {pkg} ... which no
                # consumer can restore") — two gates reading one feed must not disagree about what
                # it says.
                raise Finding(
                    f"the feed serves no stable version of {pkg} — only prereleases "
                    f"{sorted(live)} — while the registry tracks the stable channel. The coherent "
                    f"set cannot be classified against a member no consumer can restore. Publish "
                    f"the stable package, or move the registry to a prerelease channel."
                )
            live = stable
        top = newest(live)
        if parse_version(top) == want:
            at_candidate.append(pkg)
        else:
            behind.append((pkg, top))

    if len(at_candidate) == len(members):
        return "framework", [member_note, f"all {len(members)} FS.GG.UI.* members read {candidate}"]
    if not at_candidate:
        tops = {t for _, t in behind}
        note = (
            f"no FS.GG.UI.* member is at {candidate}"
            + (f"; all {len(behind)} read {tops.pop()}" if len(tops) == 1 else "")
        )
        return "template-only", [member_note, note]

    raise Finding(
        f"PARTIAL PUBLISH — {len(at_candidate)} of {len(members)} FS.GG.UI.* members are "
        f"at {candidate}, and {len(behind)} are not: "
        + ", ".join(f"{p}={t}" for p, t in sorted(behind))
        + ". That is neither a framework release nor a template-only cut, so the `version` pin "
        f"cannot be derived — it would be a guess either way. A coherent set is published at ONE "
        f"version; this one is not, which is a release defect in {PRODUCER}, not a registry defect. "
        f"Refusing to write."
    )


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `package-tag`: the field that carries "a tag is not a publish" if nobody checks it.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def verify_tags(candidate: str, kind: str, resolve_tag) -> tuple[str, list[str]]:
    """Return (package_tag, notes). Raises Finding unless the tags actually back the publish."""
    template_tag = f"fs-gg-ui-template/v{candidate}"
    shas: dict[str, str | None] = {template_tag: resolve_tag(template_tag)}

    if shas[template_tag] is None:
        raise Finding(
            f"the feed serves {TEMPLATE_PACKAGE} {candidate}, but {PRODUCER} has no tag "
            f"{template_tag!r}. `package-tag` names the tag the package was PUBLISHED from, so "
            f"there is nothing honest to write. A publish with no tag is the inverse of the 0.9.1 "
            f"phantom and is equally a release defect."
        )

    # A FRAMEWORK release cuts the full triple; a template-only cut deliberately cuts only the
    # pair (no fs-gg-ui/v* library snapshot is owed — 0.4.1's coherence guard named exactly two).
    expected = [template_tag, f"v{candidate}"]
    if kind == "framework":
        expected.insert(0, f"fs-gg-ui/v{candidate}")

    for t in expected:
        if t not in shas:
            shas[t] = resolve_tag(t)
        if shas[t] is None:
            raise Finding(
                f"{kind} release {candidate}: {PRODUCER} tag {t!r} does not exist, but the other "
                f"tags of its set do ({', '.join(k for k, v in shas.items() if v)}). An incomplete "
                f"tag set means the release did not finish as it claims. Refusing to write."
            )

    distinct = sorted(set(shas[t] for t in expected))
    if len(distinct) != 1:
        raise Finding(
            f"{kind} release {candidate}: the tag set does not resolve to ONE commit — "
            + ", ".join(f"{t}={shas[t][:8]}" for t in expected)
            + ". The tags disagree about what was released, so none of them can be cited as the "
            f"provenance of the published package. Refusing to write."
        )

    return template_tag, [f"{' -> '.join(expected)} all at {distinct[0][:8]}"]


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The write: bytes between the quotes, and nothing else.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def rewrite_field(text: str, field: str, new: str) -> tuple[str, bool]:
    """Replace CONTRACT's `field` value in `text`, preserving its trailing comment verbatim.

    Anchored inside the `- id: <CONTRACT>` block so a same-named field on another row cannot be
    hit: the search starts at the id line and stops at the next list item.
    """
    start = re.search(rf"^\s*-\s+id:\s*{re.escape(CONTRACT)}\s*$", text, re.M)
    if not start:
        raise Finding(f"cannot locate the `- id: {CONTRACT}` block to write into.")
    nxt = re.search(r"^\s*-\s+id:\s", text[start.end():], re.M)
    lo, hi = start.end(), start.end() + (nxt.start() if nxt else len(text) - start.end())

    block = text[lo:hi]
    pat = re.compile(rf'^(\s*{re.escape(field)}:\s*")([^"]*)(")', re.M)
    m = pat.search(block)
    if not m:
        raise Finding(f"cannot locate a quoted `{field}:` in the {CONTRACT} block.")
    if m.group(2) == new:
        return text, False
    block = block[:m.start()] + m.group(1) + new + m.group(3) + block[m.end():]
    return text[:lo] + block + text[hi:], True


# The mechanical head of a provenance comment: "ADVANCED <from> -> <to>", which is the clause that
# contradicts its own value when a flip lands and the prose does not follow (#856). Recognising it
# is how the bot REPORTS the rot without presuming to rewrite the judgement that follows it.
#
# The version sub-pattern may not end in `.` — it is followed by PROSE, and prose ends in a full
# stop. A greedy `[0-9A-Za-z.-]*` reads "ADVANCED 0.10.0 -> 0.11.0. FRAMEWORK MINOR" as a flip to
# `0.11.0.`, which never equals the value `0.11.0`, so EVERY correctly-written comment in the file
# would be reported as rot. The fixture caught it: a flag that fires on the happy path is the
# cry-wolf shape `skill-registry-autofix`'s header refuses, and it would have buried the ONE real
# finding this function exists to surface under two false ones.
_VER = r"\d+(?:\.\d+){0,3}(?:-[0-9A-Za-z.-]*[0-9A-Za-z])?"
_ADVANCED_RE = re.compile(rf"ADVANCED\s+(?P<from>{_VER})\s*->\s*(?P<to>{_VER})")


def stale_prose(text: str, field: str, new: str) -> str | None:
    """Return a description if `field`'s comment still describes a flip other than `-> new`."""
    start = re.search(rf"^\s*-\s+id:\s*{re.escape(CONTRACT)}\s*$", text, re.M)
    if not start:
        return None
    nxt = re.search(r"^\s*-\s+id:\s", text[start.end():], re.M)
    block = text[start.end():start.end() + (nxt.start() if nxt else len(text) - start.end())]
    m = re.search(rf'^\s*{re.escape(field)}:\s*"[^"]*"\s*#(?P<comment>.*)$', block, re.M)
    if not m:
        return None
    # Only the FIRST clause describes this flip; every later "PRIOR — ... ADVANCED" is history and
    # is correct exactly as written. Cutting at PRIOR is what keeps the bot from reporting the
    # archive as rot.
    head = m.group("comment").split("PRIOR")[0]
    adv = _ADVANCED_RE.search(head)
    if not adv:
        return None
    if adv.group("to") == new:
        return None
    return (
        f"`{field}` is now {new}, but its comment still describes "
        f"\"ADVANCED {adv.group('from')} -> {adv.group('to')}\""
    )


def reconcile(path: str, resolve_feed, resolve_nuspec, resolve_tag) -> dict:
    """Compute (and optionally apply) the flip. Returns a report dict."""
    row = load_row(path)
    pin = _require_str(row, "version")
    package = _require_str(row, "package-version")
    tag = _require_str(row, "package-tag")

    stable_channel = not is_prerelease(package)
    live = resolve_feed(TEMPLATE_PACKAGE)
    if stable_channel:
        stable = [v for v in live if not is_prerelease(v)]
        if not stable:
            raise Finding(
                f"the feed serves no stable {TEMPLATE_PACKAGE} — only prereleases {sorted(live)} — "
                f"while the registry declares the stable {package!r}. Nothing to flip TO."
            )
        live = stable
    candidate = newest(live)

    notes: list[str] = [f"{TEMPLATE_PACKAGE} newest on the org feed = {candidate}"]
    cur, want = parse_version(package), parse_version(candidate)
    if want < cur:
        raise Finding(
            f"the registry is AHEAD of the feed — `package-version` {package!r} but "
            f"{TEMPLATE_PACKAGE} newest is {candidate!r}. That is the FR-007 INVERSION: the "
            f"registry advertises a version consumers cannot restore. A bot must not 'fix' it by "
            f"winding the registry BACK — that would erase the evidence of a publish that failed or "
            f"was unlisted, and make an unrestorable pin look deliberate. A human must decide."
        )

    kind, kn = classify(candidate, resolve_feed, resolve_nuspec, stable_channel)
    notes += kn
    package_tag, tn = verify_tags(candidate, kind, resolve_tag)
    notes += tn

    # A framework release moves the pin with the package; a template-only cut decouples them and
    # the pin HOLDS. This is the whole reason classify() exists.
    new_pin = candidate if kind == "framework" else pin

    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        text = original = fh.read()

    changes: list[str] = []
    flagged: list[str] = []
    for field, old, new in (
        ("version", pin, new_pin),
        ("package-version", package, candidate),
        ("package-tag", tag, package_tag),
    ):
        text, did = rewrite_field(text, field, new)
        if did:
            changes.append(f"{field}: {old} -> {new}")
        # The prose check runs on EVERY field, changed or not: #856's `package-tag` was left
        # untouched AND its comment left describing 0.10.0, so "unchanged" is not "coherent".
        s = stale_prose(text, field, new)
        if s:
            flagged.append(s)

    return {
        "contract": CONTRACT,
        "candidate": candidate,
        "kind": kind,
        "changed": bool(changes),
        "changes": changes,
        "stale_prose": flagged,
        "notes": notes,
        "text": text,
        "original": original,
    }


def compose_body(report: dict, run_url: str) -> str:
    """The standing PR's body. Deterministic: `run_url` is injected, never read from the env.

    Says three things, in the order a reviewer needs them: what was ASSERTED (so the flip can be
    disbelieved), what was WRITTEN, and what is left for a HUMAN. The last section is the point —
    a PR that silently carried stale prose would reproduce #856 with a bot's signature on it.
    """
    kind = report["kind"]
    v = report["candidate"]
    lines = [
        f"Publish-before-flip (FR-007) step 2, for **{kind} release {v}** — opened by "
        f"`feed-autofix.yml` (.github#748).",
        "",
        "FS.GG.Rendering published; this row records it. The flip below is **derived from the "
        "live org feed**, not from a release's self-report, so it is correct for a release that "
        "was dispatched, hand-published, or re-published.",
        "",
        "## What was asserted",
        "",
    ]
    lines += [f"- {n}" for n in report["notes"]]
    lines += [
        "",
        f"Classified **{kind}**"
        + (
            " — every FS.GG.UI.* member moved, so the `version` framework pin advances with the "
            "package."
            if kind == "framework"
            else " — no FS.GG.UI.* member moved, so the `version` framework pin HOLDS and the two "
            "fields decouple (the 0.4.1 shape)."
        ),
        "",
        "## What this PR writes",
        "",
        "```",
    ]
    lines += report["changes"] or ["(no field changed)"]
    lines += ["```", ""]

    if report["stale_prose"]:
        lines += [
            "## Still needs a human — provenance prose",
            "",
            "The bot writes the three mechanical fields and **not** the provenance comments: "
            "\"what makes this a major\" is the judgement this row exists to record, and a bot "
            "must not author it.",
            "",
            "But a comment that contradicts its own value is worse than no comment, and that is "
            "not hypothetical — the last hand-flip (#856) moved `package-version` to 0.11.0 and "
            "left its comment describing the 0.10.0 flip, with nothing to catch it. So: **please "
            "rewrite these before merging.**",
            "",
        ]
        lines += [f"- [ ] {s}" for s in report["stale_prose"]]
        lines += [""]
    else:
        lines += [
            "## Provenance prose",
            "",
            "Every comment in the row agrees with the value it annotates. Nothing owed.",
            "",
        ]

    lines += [
        "## What the bot did NOT check",
        "",
        "- The `version` pin on a **template-only** cut is carried over unchanged, not verified — "
        "there is nothing on the feed to verify it against.",
        "- Whether this release *should* have happened, and what it means. That is the prose above.",
        "",
        f"Computed by [this run]({run_url}).",
        "",
        "Refs #748, #267, #250.",
    ]
    return "\n".join(lines)


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("registry", nargs="?", default="registry/dependencies.yml")
    ap.add_argument("--write", action="store_true", help="apply the flip to the registry file")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="emit the report as JSON on stdout")
    ap.add_argument("--pr-body", metavar="PATH", help="write the standing PR's body to PATH")
    ap.add_argument("--run-url", default="", help="the run URL stamped into --pr-body (injected)")
    ap.add_argument("--fixture", help="serve a canned feed+tags instead of the live ones (tests only)")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if args.fixture:
        # Mirrors check-feed-coherence.py: a hook that can silently turn this into a no-op must not
        # be reachable from a real run.
        if os.environ.get("FSGG_FEED_FIXTURE_OK") != "1":
            print(
                "feed-autofix: --fixture requires FSGG_FEED_FIXTURE_OK=1 and is for tests/ only. "
                "Refusing: a canned feed is not a coherence signal.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 2
        with open(args.fixture, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            canned = json.load(fh)
        feeds, tags = canned.get("feed", {}), canned.get("tags", {})
        # Keyed "<id>@<version>" -> the nuspec XML. The fixture serves the XML rather than a ready
        # -made id list, so every case drives the REAL nuspec parser: a fixture that handed back a
        # parsed set would leave `nuspec_dependency_ids` untested by every case but its own.
        nuspecs = canned.get("nuspec", {})

        def resolve_feed(pkg: str) -> list[str]:
            if pkg not in feeds:
                raise GateError(f"fixture serves no feed entry for {pkg!r}")
            return list(feeds[pkg])

        def resolve_nuspec(pkg: str, version: str) -> str:
            key = f"{pkg}@{version}"
            if key not in nuspecs:
                # Mirrors the live 404: a package the feed does not serve.
                raise GateError(f"fixture serves no nuspec for {key!r}")
            return nuspecs[key]

        def resolve_tag(t: str):
            return tags.get(t)
    else:
        token = os.environ.get("GITHUB_TOKEN", "")
        if not token:
            print(
                "feed-autofix: GITHUB_TOKEN is unset. The feed read needs `packages: read` and the "
                "tag read needs `contents: read` on " + PRODUCER + ". Refusing to report a flip "
                "computed from a feed it could not read.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 2

        def resolve_feed(pkg: str) -> list[str]:
            return feed_versions(pkg, token)

        def resolve_nuspec(pkg: str, version: str) -> str:
            return package_nuspec(pkg, version, token)

        def resolve_tag(t: str):
            import urllib.error
            import urllib.request

            url = f"https://api.github.com/repos/{PRODUCER}/git/ref/tags/{t}"
            req = urllib.request.Request(
                url,
                headers={
                    "Authorization": f"Bearer {token}",
                    "Accept": "application/vnd.github+json",
                    "User-Agent": "fsgg-feed-autofix",
                },
            )
            try:
                with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as resp:
                    return json.loads(resp.read().decode("utf-8"))["object"]["sha"]
            except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
                if e.code == 404:
                    return None   # the one honest "no" — every other failure raises.
                raise GateError(f"tag read for {t!r} failed: HTTP {e.code} {e.reason}") from e
            except urllib.error.URLError as e:
                raise GateError(f"{PRODUCER} unreachable reading tag {t!r}: {e.reason}") from e

    try:
        report = reconcile(args.registry, resolve_feed, resolve_nuspec, resolve_tag)
    except Finding as e:
        print(f"feed-autofix: FINDING — {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps({"changed": False, "finding": str(e)}))
        return 1
    except GateError as e:
        print(f"feed-autofix: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    text = report.pop("text")
    report.pop("original")

    if args.write and report["changed"]:
        with open(args.registry, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(text)

    if args.pr_body:
        with open(args.pr_body, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(compose_body(report, args.run_url))

    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(report, indent=2))
    else:
        for n in report["notes"]:
            print(f"  ok   {n}")
        if report["changed"]:
            print(f"\n{report['kind']} release {report['candidate']} — flip:")
            for c in report["changes"]:
                print(f"  {c}")
        else:
            print(f"\nalready coherent at {report['candidate']} ({report['kind']}).")
        for s in report["stale_prose"]:
            print(f"  NEEDS A HUMAN: {s}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
